meta {
  name: Demo Flow
  seq: 1
}

docs {
  # Live demo script

  Run the requests in this folder top to bottom. Responses feed the next
  steps automatically (created IDs are captured into runtime variables), so
  no copy-pasting is needed during the demo.

  ## The setting

  Our customer Weber builds food processing lines. The tenant is one of
  Weber's customers, `Aldi Süd – Cheese Line Nürnberg`, running a cheese
  slicing line.

  A device is **not** a machine: it is a standalone **edge IPC** in the
  line's control cabinet, running containerized apps against the machine next
  to it. The fleet is three identical IPCs of the same model, told apart by
  which machine on the line each one serves — listed here in the order the
  cheese passes through. All three sit in the group
  `Slicing Line 2 – Cheese`, so `GET /devices` reads like a production line
  rather than a lab.

  Devices have no name field in the API, so an IPC's identity lives in its
  serial number — the string on the type plate inside the cabinet door, not
  the serial of the machine it watches.

  | edge | the IPC serves | IPC serial |
  |---|---|---|
  | 1 | weSLICE 6000 slicer | `IPC427E-2024-0417` |
  | 2 | weLOAD 3000 infeed loader | `IPC427E-2024-0208` |
  | 3 | weSCAN 2100 inline scanner | `IPC427E-2025-0093` |

  ## Before the demo

  The demo operator prepares everything (API servers per run, edge VMs
  booted, the slicer and loader IPCs registered — the scanner IPC stays
  unregistered on purpose; registering it live *is* the demo) and hands you:

  - which run to use → select the matching environment
    (`demo-run1` / `demo-run2` / `demo-run3`),
  - the TPM endorsement key of the scanner IPC → paste it into the
    `edge3EndorsementKey` environment variable (see the docs on
    *04 Register Edge 3* for the exact format). Each prepared run boots its
    edges with a fresh emulated TPM, so this key changes every time and is
    never pre-filled; `edge3Serial` is already set to the fixed serial above.

  (Behind the scenes this is `demo/edge/prepare_edge_vms.sh` +
  `prepare_edge_demo.sh`.)

  ## Story

  1. **Fleet overview** (01–02): the slicer and loader IPCs are connected and
     reporting; the scanner IPC is missing from the line.
  2. **Live logs** (03): open the SSE stream in its own tab and leave it
     running — everything that follows shows up here in real time.
  3. **Bring the scanner online** (04–05): create a pending registration; the
     scanner IPC proves possession of its TPM key and self-registers.
  4. **Deploy + update an app** (06–10): push hello-world to the scanner IPC,
     then roll out a config change and watch the logs pick it up.
  5. **OS switch** (11–13): assign a new OS image; the IPC stages it, reboots
     into it with near-zero downtime, and reports back.
  6. **Audit trail** (14): every change made during the demo, who and when.
}
